WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.510
BETH: An individual with autism
who is anxious and struggling to

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.310
contain their own behaviour may not
be able to control their anxiety

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.899
and that behaviour might cause them
to develop more extreme behaviours.

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:15.650
JOE: Stimming is often very enjoyable
can be a good way for an individual with

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:18.300
autism to reduce stress and anxiety.

00:00:18.889 --> 00:00:22.340
However, stimming can sometimes
cause self-injury, such as,

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:23.750
head-banging or scratching.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:28.489
If this behaviour becomes in some way
unsafe, the individual needs to be

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:32.789
support them to stop or modify the
behaviour or reduce their reliance on it.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.780
BETH: Maybe their senses are being
overloaded more than usual or maybe

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.690
they feel pain and can’t control it.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.290
If the behaviour of the individual
cannot be de-escalated by the

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.910
usual strategies, then light
physical guidance may be needed.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.760
JOE: If they are hitting themselves,
gently moving their hand away may help.

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:54.980
If they are head banging, putting a
pillow or cushion between them and the

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.320
hard object will help to prevent further
damage to themselves and the object.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.860
Providing pain killers for physical pain
if prescribed can help to remove the

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:08.570
stress of pain and removing them to a room
where they can’t easily inflict physical

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:12.780
injury to themselves can mean there are
less danger from things around them.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.259
BETH: If a risk of serious harm exists,
then a specialist may suggest more

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.300
physical restraints such as helmets,
gloves and arm restraints, which

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.520
can reduce the sensory experience
and frequency, as well as the harm.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.390
But these should only be used in
emergencies as they don’t address

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.030
the cause of the behaviour just
alleviate the potential injury.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:37.810
JOE: When a behaviour becomes so extreme
that it causes injury to an individual or

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.760
others it is a great cause for concern.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:45.800
When an individual is engaged in an
active, disruptive stage of a behaviour,

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:50.020
such as showing aggression or anger,
the focus should be on the safety of

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.020
the individual and those around them.

